"""Original homeowner planning manuscript; all numerical rates are invented USD examples."""
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,deck,paragraphs,example,rows,conclusion,prompts,checks,task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))

chapter('Start with a roofing decision brief','Define the problem before choosing a product.',[
'Write down why the roof is being considered now: an active leak, recurring repairs, planned ownership, documented damage or a broader renovation. Those triggers lead to different decisions. An old roof is a reason to investigate, not proof that every layer must be replaced. Ask a qualified roofing professional to explain the observed condition and the realistic repair and replacement options.',
'Keep the record useful to someone who has not visited the house. Include the address, occupied areas, known roof history, prior invoices and dated photos taken from a safe location. Record what you observed separately from what a contractor diagnosed. A stain on a ceiling identifies a symptom; it does not identify the entry point or establish the correct repair.',
'Agree on a working spending ceiling and the decision makers before inviting bids. List required outcomes such as resolving water entry, retaining a particular appearance or coordinating solar removal. Rank optional upgrades separately. Keep urgent temporary protection as a distinct approved service so it does not silently become authorization for the entire replacement.',
'This handbook organizes the owner side of the job. It never asks you to climb onto a roof, open a roof assembly or inspect a hazardous attic. Use contractor measurements and professional reports for those tasks. Start with the first eight chapters, then use the technical scope sheets as questions for the responsible contractor.'
], 'An owner can allocate $22,000 but sets a working ceiling of $20,000. An agreed $600 temporary protection visit is part of that project ceiling. No replacement contract has yet been approved.', [('Available project funds','$22,000'),('Working ceiling including protection','$20,000'),('Protection already authorized','$600'),('Ceiling left for remaining work','$19,400')],
'The unallocated $2,000 outside the working ceiling is not a second project reserve. Record any later change to the ceiling explicitly.',
['Reason for project / observed symptoms / photo references','Property and roof history / records available','Required outcomes / optional upgrades / exclusions','Working ceiling / available funds / approvers','Assessment needed / responsible professional / date'],
['Symptoms and diagnoses are recorded separately.','Temporary protection has a defined authorization.','No owner roof access is required.'], 'Create the brief and send the same version to each bidder.')

chapter('Read the roof as an assembly','The covering is one part of the water-management system.',[
'Organize the proposal by layers and interfaces: existing covering, deck, underlayment, selected covering, edges, penetrations, flashing and ventilation where applicable. A shingle color is easy to compare; an omitted flashing transition is easier to miss. Ask which components remain, which are replaced and who verifies compatibility.',
'The same product name does not establish the same job. One proposal might include full removal, new flashings and cleanup; another might assume reuse and limited repair. Identify the scope before comparing rates. Ask the contractor to tie specifications to the actual roof configuration and current manufacturer instructions, with local requirements checked by the responsible professional.',
'Draw a simple owner reference sketch or annotate a contractor drawing. Give each roof section an ID so photos, measurements, exclusions and changes can be traced to a location. Separate the garage or porch if it has a different slope or assembly. A single whole-house total can hide work that belongs to a different system.',
'Record responsibility at boundaries with siding, masonry, skylights, gutters and solar equipment. Who removes an item, who reinstalls it and who handles finish restoration? A complete interface assignment is more useful than a promise that the contractor will take care of everything. Leave any unverified condition visibly unresolved.'
], 'For a teaching comparison, a roof assembly line includes $10,200 for removal and covering, $1,100 for flashing and $700 for edge and penetration details. These invented amounts exclude other project costs.', [('Removal and covering','$10,200'),('Flashing scope','$1,100'),('Edges and penetrations','$700'),('Defined assembly subtotal','$12,000')],
'Do not add flashing again if the $10,200 line already includes it. This subtotal is not a full project budget or a local market price.',
['Roof sections / drawing IDs / assembly type','Layers removed / retained / replaced','Covering and accessory product specifications','Interfaces with adjacent trades / responsibility','Unverified conditions and evidence needed'],
['Retained components are named.','Interfaces have one accountable party.','No installation design is inferred from this worksheet.'], 'Ask each bidder to mark the assembly items included in the base price.')

chapter('Establish area and roofing squares','Keep measured roof surface separate from the house footprint.',[
'A roofing square is 100 square feet of roof surface. The house floor area is a different quantity. Overhangs, multiple stories, attached structures and slope can make a floor-area shortcut misleading. Ask for the measurement source, date and assumptions, and distinguish a preliminary owner estimate from the installer-confirmed takeoff.',
'For a simple roof plane, surface area can be found from its projected horizontal area multiplied by a slope factor. With pitch p inches of rise per 12 inches of run, the factor is the square root of 1 plus (p divided by 12) squared. This works only when the projected area and pitch describe the same plane; calculate different planes separately.',
'An aerial report may provide useful dimensions, but its report date and limitations still matter. Ask who resolves discrepancies before ordering. Dormers, valleys and details can affect cutting and labor even when net surface area barely changes. Record quantities used for material ordering separately from those used for an installed contract rate.',
'Use measurements supplied by a professional or a suitable report. Do not climb to obtain pitch or confirm a dimension. The arithmetic in this chapter checks the relationship between units; it cannot verify framing, structural capacity, workmanship or the suitability of any roof system.'
], 'A simple plane has a 1,600 sq ft horizontal projection and a 6:12 pitch. The example excludes separate porches, overhang adjustments and other planes. The factor is approximately 1.118034.', [('Slope factor: sqrt(1 + (6/12)^2)','1.118034'),('Surface: 1,600 × 1.118034','1,788.85 sq ft'),('Squares: 1,788.85 / 100','17.89 squares'),('Order allowance','Not yet added')],
'Do not apply the slope factor again to a report that already gives true roof surface area. Keep unrounded values until the final planning result.',
['Measurement provider / report date / limitations','Plane IDs / projected or surface area clearly labeled','Pitch for each applicable plane / source','Calculated surface area / squares / exclusions','Installer-confirmed quantity / discrepancy resolution'],
['Surface area and footprint are distinguished.','Slope has been applied at most once.','No unsafe owner measurement is requested.'], 'Check the units with the roof-pitch and roofing-square calculators on HomeCostEngine.')

chapter('Order quantity, packages and waste','An ordering allowance needs a reason and an owner.',[
'Separate the net installed area, the chosen cutting allowance and the final packaged purchase quantity. Complex geometry, product dimensions and layout can change material needs. A blanket percentage is a planning assumption until the installer confirms it for the selected product. Do not treat a convenient example percentage as an industry requirement.',
'Ask how starter, hip and ridge products are quantified. Some products are sold separately and should not be inferred from field-shingle coverage. Package coverage varies, so use the actual manufacturer label rather than assuming that every bundle contains the same area. Record units for trim, fasteners and accessories separately.',
'Round up at the packaging stage, after computing the allowance. Preserve both the unrounded calculation and the ordered quantity so a supplier or installer can check it. Confirm how special-order items, unopened returns, damaged deliveries and retained spare materials are handled. A return credit is not available cash until accepted.',
'Clarify who owns the ordering decision and bears the consequences of a shortage or excess. If the contractor prices a fixed installed job, do not add their purchase allowance as a second owner cost. Record owner-supplied material separately because responsibility for damage, compatibility and warranty may change.'
], 'Net field area is 1,800 sq ft. A chosen 10% cutting allowance gives 1,980 sq ft. This illustrative product label says 33 sq ft per bundle. Starter and ridge products are separate.', [('Net field area','1,800 sq ft'),('With 10% allowance','1,980 sq ft'),('Bundles: 1,980 / 33','60 bundles'),('Packaged field coverage','1,980 sq ft')],
'The 10% assumption and 33 sq ft label are example inputs, not universal product specifications. Have the installer confirm accessories and order quantities.',
['Selected product / actual package coverage / units','Net area / verified measurement reference','Allowance percentage / reason / approving installer','Rounded package counts / separate accessory quantities','Ordering responsibility / return policy / spare stock'],
['Package coverage comes from the selected product.','Accessories are not silently included.','The same waste cost is not counted twice.'], 'Ask the bidder for the takeoff basis, not just the total bundle count.')

chapter('Compare roof system options','Choose on compatible scope and ownership priorities.',[
'Compare plausible systems for this building rather than collecting unrelated product prices. Asphalt shingles, metal, tile and low-slope systems can differ in assembly, support needs, detailing, maintenance and installer availability. Ask a qualified professional which options suit the roof and what additional design or verification each option requires.',
'Create a comparison with the same removal, preparation, accessory, permit and cleanup boundaries. A covering-only quote cannot be compared directly with a complete assembly. Record the exact product, proposed assembly, stated exclusions and schedule. Keep any structural review or adjacent-trade work visible rather than assuming it is included in a material upgrade.',
'Discuss practical ownership factors: appearance restrictions, noise expectations, access for future equipment work, repair availability and documented maintenance obligations. Product marketing and broad lifetime claims are not a guarantee for your property. Separate product warranty terms from the contractor promise to correct installation problems.',
'An incremental-cost comparison can clarify a choice, but dividing a premium by an assumed life does not establish a payback or resale gain. Label those assumptions and avoid presenting an invented useful life as a forecast. Choose the option whose full scope, obligations and tradeoffs you understand.'
], 'Two scope-matched fictional proposals are $16,000 and $21,000. The owner plans to stay for ten years and wants to understand the extra initial cost, without assuming resale value or maintenance savings.', [('Option A installed scope','$16,000'),('Option B same-scope alternative','$21,000'),('Extra initial cost','$5,000'),('Premium / 10 planned years','$500 per year')],
'The annualized premium is only a way to view initial spending. It ignores financing, maintenance, future prices and resale; it is not an investment return.',
['Options suitable for this roof / professional confirmation','Exact product and complete assembly per option','Matched inclusions / exclusions / design reviews','Initial premium / maintenance obligations / priorities','Decision and assumptions that could change it'],
['Systems are compared on the same scope.','Lifetime claims are identified as claims.','Structural suitability has an accountable verifier.'], 'Ask two bidders to price the same preferred system before comparing a premium alternative.')

chapter('Tear-off and disposal without scope gaps','Removal is a service with quantity and access assumptions.',[
'A removal proposal should identify the known layers, areas included, disposal, property protection and the handling of unexpected conditions. A roof re-cover is a different scope from full tear-off. Ask whether the existing condition and proposed approach are suitable under manufacturer instructions and local requirements; this book does not authorize a re-cover.',
'Record how additional layers or unusual material will be priced if discovered. An owner allowance is not a permission slip for undefined work. Agree the evidence and written approval process before demolition. Where suspect hazardous material is identified, ask for qualified assessment and appropriate handling rather than assuming ordinary removal labor applies.',
'Disposal costs may depend on container placement, weight allowances, haul distance and access. Ask which fees are included and who manages any permits for placement. Confirm driveway protection, daily debris handling, nail cleanup and what happens if the container cannot be positioned as assumed.',
'Temporary weather protection belongs in the removal plan. Ask what area may be opened at once, who decides whether conditions permit work and who responds if water enters before completion. The owner needs contacts and responsibilities, not instructions for working on the roof.'
], 'An illustrative 18-square tear-off allowance uses $65 per square for removal and $550 for disposal. Both are invented planning inputs. The disposal fee is expressly excluded from the removal rate.', [('Removal: 18 × $65','$1,170'),('Disposal allowance','$550'),('Removal plus disposal','$1,720'),('Extra layers','Not priced here')],
'If disposal is already inside the removal rate, omit the separate $550 line. Extra layers need a separately documented basis and approval.',
['Known covering / layer count / confirmation method','Tear-off versus re-cover / professional approval','Removal unit / rate inclusions / extra-layer rule','Container / access / protection / disposal fees','Temporary weather protection / contacts / responsibilities'],
['Disposal is counted once.','Unknown layers have a written change process.','Property protection and weather responsibility are clear.'], 'Have the contractor explain the tear-off and protection plan in writing.')

chapter('Decking and concealed-condition allowances','Agree the repair unit before the condition is exposed.',[
'Deck condition may not be fully visible until the covering is removed. Ask how the contractor documents damage and who decides what work is required. A moisture stain, softness report or photograph should be tied to a location and professional assessment. Do not interpret a budget allowance as a diagnosis or a structural repair design.',
'A repair rate needs a unit and scope: per sheet, per square foot or another defined basis. Identify material, thickness where specified by the responsible professional, removal, fastening, labor and disposal. Nominal sheet dimensions do not prove that the whole sheet can be used in the actual layout.',
'Compare rates only after their boundaries match. A low material-only number is not comparable to installed repair. Ask how partial sheets, minimum charges and framing repairs are treated. Framing work should not be silently included in a generic decking line if it requires different evaluation or pricing.',
'Set an owner approval process with photos, quantity, rate and revised total before work proceeds when practicable. Record the contractual procedure for urgent protection if the owner cannot be reached. Protect the reserve from arbitrary spending: release funds against documented scope, then update the remaining risk.'
], 'The teaching repair is six full 4 ft × 8 ft sheets at $115 per installed sheet. A comparison bidder quotes $3.80 per sq ft for identical scope. Both use full-sheet coverage solely for this example.', [('Area: 6 × 32','192 sq ft'),('Bid A: 6 × $115','$690'),('Bid B: 192 × $3.80','$729.60'),('Difference for matched scope','$39.60')],
'Confirm actual cut layout, minimum charges and material specification. The conversion does not establish a fair market rate or the extent of damage.',
['Observed condition / location / evidence / assessor','Specified repair material and complete included scope','Unit / coverage / minimum charge / agreed rate','Quantity approval / change reference / owner contact','Allowance used / remaining reserve / unresolved risk'],
['Per-sheet and per-area rates use matching scope.','Framing work is separately identified.','Repair quantities are evidenced before reconciliation.'], 'Request a sample concealed-condition change notice before signing the contract.')

chapter('Underlayment and water-control details','Ask how the layers work together at this roof.',[
'Underlayment and special water-control membranes should be identified by product and coverage area in the proposal. Avoid the vague phrase premium underlayment without a specification. Ask the contractor how the proposed components match the covering, deck condition, slope and current local requirements.',
'Some coverage may be specified at eaves, valleys or particular transitions; the extent depends on the project and responsible design. This handbook provides no installation layout or code minimum. Ask for an annotated scope showing included areas, overlaps and penetrations as established by the professional and manufacturer instructions.',
'Separate material quantity from installed labor pricing. Rolls, laps and coverage instructions affect an order, while an installed contract may already cover those tasks. Record who verifies the final quantities and how substitutions are approved. A named product should not be replaced by an unspecified equivalent without a documented decision.',
'Discuss exposure and scheduling limits with the contractor. Temporary protection requirements and product storage matter when weather or delivery changes. Keep product data and photographs in the project folder so later questions can be connected to the actual materials used.'
], 'A hypothetical specialty membrane area is 240 sq ft. The chosen planning allowance is 15%, giving 276 sq ft. The illustrative roll has 200 sq ft of usable coverage after the specified application assumptions.', [('Required planned coverage','276 sq ft'),('Roll count: round up 276 / 200','2 rolls'),('Two rolls at invented $95 each','$190'),('Installation','Excluded from this example')],
'Use the selected product instructions for coverage. Do not infer usable coverage from gross roll dimensions or assume the example allowance is correct for your roof.',
['Product names / compatible covering / specification source','Included areas / drawing / professional requirement','Coverage per package / laps already allowed for','Installed versus material-only price / substitutions','Storage / exposure plan / photographs to retain'],
['Coverage is specific to the selected product.','Local requirements are professionally verified.','The example is not used as an installation specification.'], 'Ask for a written component schedule with proposed substitutions identified.')

chapter('Flashing, edges and penetrations','Small interfaces deserve explicit lines in the quote.',[
'Map the roof intersections with walls, chimneys, skylights, pipes and other penetrations. Ask which flashings will be replaced, which retained and why. New covering does not by itself explain how a reused transition will be assessed or warranted. Tie the decision to location IDs and documented condition.',
'Measure and price using appropriate units: linear feet of edge, counts of pipe details or a defined complete chimney scope. A number of penetrations does not describe complexity on its own. Access, adjacent finishes and the extent of restoration can change the work substantially.',
'Coordinate roof work with masonry, siding and equipment contractors when needed. Clarify who opens and restores adjacent surfaces, and who checks the completed interface. Avoid a responsibility gap where the roofer excludes masonry work and the owner assumes it is part of the roof contract.',
'Ask how the contractor documents concealed work before it is covered. Photos can support the record, but do not replace professional inspection or acceptance. Save product details, location references and any agreed exclusions with the warranty information.'
], 'For fictional quote normalization, three pipe details cost $140 each and a separate chimney interface costs $650. These are scope-matched installed allowances, not prices to use without a local quote.', [('Pipe details: 3 × $140','$420'),('Defined chimney interface','$650'),('Combined allowance','$1,070'),('Masonry repair','Excluded')],
'If a bid includes the pipe details, do not add them again. Obtain a separate assessment and price for excluded masonry work.',
['Penetration and intersection IDs / photos / quantities','Replace or retain / condition evidence / verifier','Exact included flashing and adjacent restoration scope','Trade responsibility / exclusions / acceptance record','Unit prices / allowance / approved changes'],
['Every major interface has a scope decision.','Adjacent-trade restoration is assigned.','Reused components have documented reasoning.'], 'Walk through the interface list with the contractor using drawings and safe ground-level views.')

chapter('Ventilation, insulation and moisture questions','Do not treat an attic symptom as a vent-count formula.',[
'Ask the contractor to identify the existing roof and attic arrangement before proposing ventilation changes. Vented and unvented assemblies involve different considerations. More vents alone do not establish a correct solution, and a product count cannot diagnose moisture, air leakage or insulation problems.',
'Record the observed symptom, the assessor, proposed remedy and evidence. Moisture in an attic may involve multiple sources and pathways. Have the appropriate professional explain what will be changed and what remains outside the roofing scope. Do not cover a known concern with a cosmetic repair and assume the cause is resolved.',
'For any proposed ventilation work, request the design basis, product names, intake and exhaust scope and installation responsibility. Manufacturer instructions and applicable local requirements belong with the professional review. This chapter intentionally provides no vent-sizing rule or instructions for attic entry.',
'If insulation or air-sealing work is proposed separately, coordinate responsibilities and timing. Retain the assessment, approved scope, photographs and completion record. Keep the energy claim distinct from moisture control: an upgrade may have one purpose without proving the other.'
], 'A hypothetical budget has $800 for an agreed ventilation scope and $450 for a separately assessed air-sealing task. The example simply records two approved scope lines; it does not size either intervention.', [('Defined ventilation work','$800'),('Separate air-sealing work','$450'),('Combined agreed scope','$1,250'),('Unassessed moisture repair','Not included')],
'The cost addition is valid only when tasks do not overlap. Have the professionals coordinate the assembly and confirm the proposed work addresses the diagnosed issue.',
['Existing assembly / assessor / report date','Observed moisture or comfort issue / supporting evidence','Proposed solution / design basis / product schedule','Roofing versus insulation-trade responsibilities','Completion evidence / follow-up / unresolved issues'],
['A professional has identified the assembly.','The remedy is tied to an assessed cause.','No generic vent count is treated as design.'], 'Ask who owns the complete moisture-control question, including work outside the roof contract.')

chapter('Cool roofs and optional energy upgrades','Separate a plausible benefit from a promised saving.',[
'A reflective roof can reduce solar heat absorption, but the household benefit depends on climate, insulation, roof configuration and heating and cooling use. The U.S. Department of Energy discusses these tradeoffs in its cool-roof guidance [3]. Use a local assessment and selected product data rather than assuming one saving applies everywhere.',
'Compare the incremental cost against an otherwise matching roof scope. A whole replacement is usually not the right numerator when the decision is only whether to choose a reflective option during an already-planned replacement. Record maintenance requirements, appearance restrictions and warranty compatibility alongside any modeled energy estimate.',
'Simple payback equals the incremental initial cost divided by a stated annual saving. It ignores financing, maintenance, price changes and the timing of benefits. If no credible annual estimate is available, write unknown rather than inventing a percentage reduction in utility bills.',
'Coordinate solar removal or future installation separately. Ask the qualified providers about mounting, weatherproofing responsibilities, warranty implications and scheduling. A planned roof replacement is a useful coordination point, but it does not prove that solar or another upgrade is suitable or economical for this property.'
], 'A scope-matched reflective option adds a hypothetical $1,200. A separate, property-specific model is assumed to estimate $100 annual net savings for teaching arithmetic only.', [('Incremental initial cost','$1,200'),('Assumed annual net savings','$100'),('Simple payback: 1,200 / 100','12 years'),('If savings were only $60','20 years')],
'These savings are invented, not a forecast. Do not advertise a payback without documented local assumptions and an explanation of excluded costs.',
['Upgrade option / matched baseline / increment','Climate and property inputs / model / source date','Annual savings estimate / uncertainty / exclusions','Warranty / maintenance / equipment coordination','Decision rationale beyond simple payback'],
['Only the incremental upgrade cost is compared.','Energy assumptions have a source or are marked unknown.','No rebate or tax benefit is assumed without verification.'], 'Read the DOE source note and ask for a property-specific comparison before paying a premium.')

chapter('Site logistics and weather planning','Access assumptions can change both price and schedule.',[
'Record delivery access, driveway limits, overhead obstacles, container location, occupied entrances and any shared-property constraints. Ask the contractor to assess the route and protection requirements. A truck shown on a standard proposal is not proof that it can use this driveway or that the proposed unloading area is appropriate.',
'Agree who moves vehicles, protects landscaping, coordinates pets and provides access to necessary areas. Put dates and contact names beside those tasks. Discuss noise and work hours using local and contractual requirements. A practical plan protects people and property without turning the owner into the site safety supervisor.',
'Weather can alter sequencing and temporary protection needs. Ask who decides when work stops, how exposed areas are protected and how delays are communicated. Record the contact for urgent problems and the process for documenting water entry or property damage; do not attempt temporary roof work yourself.',
'Keep equipment, permits and restoration responsibilities visible. Where access is constrained, request a price for the required alternative before signing. A logistics allowance should identify what would use it and who approves additional spending.'
], 'A fictional proposal excludes a $300 container placement charge and $450 for approved driveway protection. Both are documented separately and are not in the base price.', [('Container placement','$300'),('Driveway protection','$450'),('Known logistics additions','$750'),('Unplanned weather delay','Unpriced risk')],
'Known logistics additions belong in the base plan. A reserve for uncertain delays is a separate decision, not a reason to count the $750 twice.',
['Delivery / container / driveway / shared access limits','Protection / occupants / pets / work-hour plan','Weather stop decision / temporary protection / contacts','Permits and restoration responsibilities','Known extra costs / remaining uncertainty / approvals'],
['The contractor has assessed actual access.','Emergency contacts are available.','Site safety remains with the responsible contractor.'], 'Request a one-page access and weather plan with the accepted proposal.')

chapter('Permits, approvals and inspection timing','Assign the paperwork before scheduling the work.',[
'Ask the responsible local authority and contractor which permits, inspections or other approvals apply to this specific roof scope. Do not assume that a repair, re-cover and full replacement share the same process. This handbook gives no universal permit rule, code minimum or inspection schedule.',
'Put application responsibility, fees and inspection coordination in the contract scope. Record the authority contact, submission reference and any conditions that affect materials or timing. If a homeowners association or another property body needs to review appearance, keep that approval separate from the local permit process; one does not establish the other.',
'Ask what evidence must be available before concealed work is covered and who coordinates that decision. The contractor should explain the planned sequence and how required checks fit it. Do not treat a delivery date or an owner photograph as proof that the appropriate approval has been obtained.',
'Keep known administrative costs separate from uncertain delays. An allowance for a fee is not evidence that the permit has been issued. Where the fee or process is not confirmed, name the person who will resolve it and the deadline before the project commits to a dependent activity.'
], 'A hypothetical project includes a confirmed $280 application fee and $120 inspection administration charge, both excluded from the contractor base price. A separate association review has no confirmed charge yet.', [('Application fee','$280'),('Inspection administration','$120'),('Known administrative costs','$400'),('Association review charge','Unknown; confirm')],
'Include the known $400 once. Mark the unresolved review charge as an open item rather than writing zero and treating the budget as complete.',
['Authority / scope / permit determination source and date','Application owner / reference / confirmed fees','Required inspections / sequence / coordinating person','Separate property approvals / restrictions / status','Open fees or conditions / verifier / decision deadline'],
['Administrative responsibility is assigned.','An allowance is not mistaken for an issued approval.','Dependent work is coordinated with required checks.'], 'Ask for the permit and inspection plan alongside the proposed work schedule.')

chapter('Prequalify the contractor','Verify evidence before comparing a polished proposal.',[
'Ask for the business identity, physical contact details, references, required licenses and relevant insurance documentation. NRCA consumer guidance describes contractor qualification checks [2]. Verify project-specific requirements through the responsible licensing or local authority; a website badge or association membership alone does not establish permission to perform this job.',
'Check insurance evidence for the work period with the appropriate source and ask who supervises the project. Identify the contractual party, subcontracting arrangements and day-to-day contact. If the person selling the roof differs from the installing company, clarify who is accountable for scope, payments and warranty service.',
'Ask references about comparable work, communication, site care and the handling of changes rather than requesting a general star rating. A reference is useful evidence, not a guarantee. Keep dated notes and distinguish statements you verified from claims still awaiting documentation.',
'The FTC recommends checking contractors, getting written estimates and reviewing a written contract before work starts [1]. Pressure to sign immediately is a reason to pause and verify the offer. Obtain appropriate independent advice for contract or financing questions rather than letting a sales deadline replace review.'
], 'Bid A and Bid B are both nominally $17,500. Only A has supplied the requested documents. The owner records B as incomplete rather than inventing a monetary penalty or awarding an unsupported rating.', [('Bid A / quoted total','$17,500'),('Bid B / quoted total','$17,500'),('A qualification record','Evidence received'),('B qualification record','Pending verification')],
'A missing document is a decision gate. It is not proof of wrongdoing, and it should not be disguised as an arbitrary discount or star score.',
['Contracting business / supervisor / contact details','License requirement / verification source / date','Insurance evidence / applicable coverage period','Comparable references / questions / dated notes','Outstanding documents / decision gate / deadline'],
['The installing and contracting parties are identified.','Claims are separated from verified evidence.','No invented customer review is used as proof.'], 'Complete the qualification record before choosing between price-matched proposals.')

chapter('Normalize the bids line by line','The cheapest headline can describe a smaller job.',[
'Use one row per scope item and one column per bidder. Mark included, excluded, allowance or unclear. Begin with area, system, removal, deck repair, flashing, ventilation, logistics, permits and closeout. A comparison is only as complete as the unanswered rows; do not convert silence into an inclusion.',
'Use documented comparison adjustments for excluded work. A separate quote or written clarification is stronger than an owner guess. Clearly label an adjusted comparison as your analysis, not a changed offer from the bidder. Confirm tax treatment, allowance boundaries and expiration dates before ranking totals.',
'Separate monetary differences from service differences. Warranty obligations, supervision, communication and schedule matter, but arbitrary point scores can hide judgment. Explain the tradeoff in words. A longer quoted warranty term is not automatically better if exclusions or claim obligations differ.',
'Send unresolved questions to bidders in the same format and retain their answers. If one revises the scope, update every comparison column to the same version before choosing. A short clarification round can be more useful than adding another unmatched proposal to the folder.'
], 'Fictional Bid A is $15,900, excluding $700 disposal and $400 permit handling supported by separate written quotes. Bid B is $16,600 including both. Other scope is assumed equal only for this example.', [('Bid A as quoted','$15,900'),('Documented missing scope','$1,100'),('Adjusted comparison A','$17,000'),('Comparable Bid B','$16,600'),('B lower after normalization','$400')],
'The adjustment changes the comparison, not the original contract offer. Resolve every other material scope difference before selecting the contractor.',
['Common scope version / bidders / quote dates','Included / excluded / allowance / unclear by item','Evidence for each comparison adjustment','Comparable totals / tax / expiry / unknowns','Non-price differences / questions / selection rationale'],
['Unclear items remain visible.','Adjustments are documented and counted once.','Bidder responses use the same scope version.'], 'Use the contractor-quote comparison calculator to check the addition, then retain written clarifications.')

chapter('Build the all-in roofing budget','Known costs and uncertainty need different buckets.',[
'Start with the accepted base scope, then add unresolved selection allowances and known owner-side costs not already included. Keep optional upgrades visible. Add a separate reserve with a named basis and purpose. A percentage chosen for an example is not a recommended reserve for every roof.',
'Map who pays each line. A permit in the contractor proposal should not also appear as an owner-side fee. The same applies to disposal, delivery and tax. If a quote is tax-inclusive, retain that fact in the comparison rather than adding a blanket tax line to everything.',
'Use a risk register for unknown quantities or conditions. Record the trigger, potential response, evidence needed and decision owner. A reserve is funding capacity for unresolved risk, not an automatic addition to the contractor invoice. Only an approved change converts part of the reserve into committed scope.',
'Compare the all-in plan with the working ceiling. If it exceeds available funding, change the scope, timing or funding explicitly. Do not remove a reserve simply to hide the gap while the same uncertainty remains. Keep a dated baseline so later changes have something meaningful to compare against.'
], 'A fictional plan has $16,000 base work, $800 known owner costs and $1,200 deck allowance. A chosen 10% reserve is applied to base work plus deck allowance only, a basis of $17,200.', [('Base work','$16,000'),('Owner-side costs','$800'),('Deck allowance','$1,200'),('Reserve: 10% × $17,200','$1,720'),('All-in plan','$19,720')],
'Against a $20,000 ceiling the plan has $280 headroom. The unused deck allowance and reserve are not automatically payable to the contractor.',
['Base / allowances / upgrades / owner costs','Payment owner and inclusion check for every line','Risk register / reserve basis / chosen amount','All-in plan / working ceiling / headroom or gap','Baseline date / decisions / funding confirmation'],
['No known cost is duplicated.','The reserve basis can be reproduced.','The plan fits funding or records a required decision.'], 'Complete the budget before paying a deposit or committing to a material upgrade.')

chapter('Contract, milestones and payment records','A payment schedule is not a substitute for completion evidence.',[
'The written contract should identify scope, price, allowances, exclusions, dates, change procedure, payment milestones and warranty responsibilities. Have the responsible adviser review terms you do not understand. Rules about deposits, cancellation rights and lien documents vary; this book does not supply a legal contract or a jurisdiction-wide payment rule.',
'Tie milestones to a clearly described event and the required evidence rather than a vague percentage complete. Ask how delays, disputed work and changes are recorded. Check invoices against the agreed schedule and retain proof of payment. A supplier delivery or crew arrival may not mean the same thing as a completed contracted milestone.',
'Keep approved budget, total commitments, paid amounts and unpaid balance in separate columns. Paid cost is already inside the full committed amount. Adding the complete contract to payments made against it inflates the forecast and can make a project appear over budget for the wrong reason.',
'Use the payment method and protections appropriate to the transaction, and keep the contracting business identity consistent across documents. Questions about financing terms, liens, releases or insurance should go to the relevant professional. Do not let an urgent payment request override unresolved identity or scope discrepancies.'
], 'A fictional $18,000 contract has $4,500 paid, leaving $13,500 unpaid. Separate owner costs of $800 remain uncommitted. The forecast excludes reserve to show the payment relationship clearly.', [('Committed contract','$18,000'),('Paid against that contract','$4,500'),('Unpaid contract balance','$13,500'),('Uncommitted owner costs','$800'),('Forecast: paid + unpaid + owner','$18,800')],
'Do not calculate $18,000 + $4,500 + $800. That counts the same $4,500 twice. Add any remaining reserve separately when preparing the complete funding forecast.',
['Contract version / agreed scope / adviser questions','Milestone / evidence / due date / amount','Paid invoices / proof / remaining obligations','Owner costs / reserve / complete forecast','Disputes or document requests / responsible person'],
['Paid and unpaid contract amounts reconcile.','Milestone evidence is defined.','Local legal questions go to a qualified adviser.'], 'Create the payment ledger before the first invoice and update it after every approved transaction.')

chapter('Changes, insurance records and scope control','A new condition needs a new documented decision.',[
'For a change, record the original scope, observed condition, evidence, proposed remedy, cost difference and schedule effect. Identify whether the amount is an addition, a credit or a transfer from an existing allowance. Ask for owner approval using the contractual process before changed work proceeds where practicable.',
'Reserve use and allowance use are different. If an approved repair is already covered by a deck allowance, reconcile that allowance rather than also charging the reserve. If the repair exceeds it, only the documented overage increases the forecast. Record credits for deleted work with the same care as additions.',
'For an insurance-related project, keep the contractor scope, insurer correspondence, payments and owner obligations in separate records. Coverage and payment timing depend on the specific policy and claim. Do not treat the contractor price as a promise that an insurer will pay it, and do not assume an insurer payment settles every project obligation.',
'Photograph and retain records safely through the professionals doing the work. Do not enter hazardous areas to create evidence. When the scope or insurer position is disputed, seek the appropriate independent assistance. The aim of this sheet is an auditable cost trail, not claim negotiation or legal advice.'
], 'The budget contains a $1,200 deck allowance. The approved actual deck repair is $1,450. A separate deleted accessory earns a $100 credit. Both changes are agreed in writing.', [('Actual repair minus allowance','$250'),('Approved deleted-work credit','-$100'),('Net forecast increase','$150'),('Remaining reserve if previously $1,720','$1,570')],
'The $1,450 repair is not added on top of the full $1,200 allowance. Replace the allowance with actual cost, then apply the separate credit once.',
['Change ID / location / evidence / original scope','Price basis / added work / deleted work / credit','Allowance replaced / net forecast effect','Owner approval / schedule change / reserve balance','Insurance correspondence kept separate / open questions'],
['Only the allowance overage increases the forecast.','Credits are recorded as well as additions.','Insurance expectations are not presented as confirmed funding.'], 'Reconcile each change against the original allowance and update the complete forecast.')

chapter('Closeout, warranties and future maintenance','Finish with a usable roof record, not only a final receipt.',[
'Before closeout, compare completed scope with the contract and approved changes. Ask the responsible contractor or inspector for the completion evidence applicable to this job. Record remaining items, the person responsible and a target date. An owner visual check is not a technical inspection or a substitute for required approvals.',
'Collect product names, invoices, completion photographs, required inspection records and warranty documents. Distinguish manufacturer coverage from workmanship coverage, including registration, maintenance, transfer and claim procedures. Save the actual terms rather than relying on the longest number in a sales brochure.',
'Reconcile the ledger: paid amounts plus unpaid obligations plus any remaining owner work. Confirm how unused allowances and credits are settled under the contract. The unused reserve is available funding, not an invoice item. Write a short explanation of the final difference from the original baseline.',
'Create a future maintenance reminder based on the installed system and professional guidance. Keep observations from safe ground-level locations and use qualified help for roof access or repairs. Store the project file where a future owner or service provider can find the relevant information without reconstructing the whole job.'
], 'The original all-in plan was $19,720. At closeout, paid costs are $17,900 and unpaid obligations are $800. No other work remains. The funding ceiling is $20,000.', [('Paid plus unpaid: 17,900 + 800','$18,700'),('Baseline minus final cost','$1,020 below plan'),('Ceiling minus final cost','$1,300 unspent'),('Unpaid obligations still to settle','$800')],
'The $800 remains an obligation even though the project is physically complete. The $1,300 funding headroom is not additional contractor compensation.',
['Completed scope / punch list / inspection references','Products / photos / warranties / registration evidence','Paid / unpaid / allowance credits / final forecast','Final variance from baseline / explanation','Maintenance guidance / contacts / record location'],
['Unpaid obligations are still visible.','Both product and workmanship documents are saved.','Future maintenance follows installed-system guidance.'], 'Keep one closeout folder with the baseline, approved changes, final ledger and warranty contacts.')

